
---
import BaseLayout from "../../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";
import Container from "../../components/ui/Container.astro";
import ModuleQuiz from "../../components/quiz/ModuleQuiz.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Introduction to Conservation | Heritage Lens"
  description="An introduction to the meaning, scope, principles and professional practice of cultural heritage conservation."
>
  <Container>
    <article class="lesson-page">

      <header class="lesson-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">
          CONSERVATION / MODULE 01
        </p>
        <h1>Introduction to Conservation.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Understanding what conservation means, why it matters
          and how we make responsible decisions about the
          preservation of cultural heritage.
        </p>
        <div class="lesson-meta">
          <span>FOUNDATION LEVEL</span>
          <span>INTRODUCTORY LESSON</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="lesson-layout">
        <aside class="lesson-sidebar">
          <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS LESSON</p>
          <nav>
            <a href="#objectives">Learning objectives</a>
            <a href="#meaning">What is conservation?</a>
            <a href="#approaches">Three approaches</a>
            <a href="#scope">Scope of conservation</a>
            <a href="#decision-making">Making decisions</a>
            <a href="#reflection">Think and reflect</a>
            <a href="#references">Further reading</a>
            <a href="#knowledge-check">Test your knowledge</a>
          </nav>
        </aside>

        <div class="lesson-content">

          <section id="objectives" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">01 / LEARNING OBJECTIVES</p>
            <h2>What will you learn?</h2>
            <p>
              By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>Explain the meaning and purpose of conservation.</li>
              <li>Distinguish preventive conservation, remedial conservation and restoration.</li>
              <li>Recognise the different factors that influence the condition of cultural heritage.</li>
              <li>Understand why conservation involves ethical and interdisciplinary decision-making.</li>
              <li>Recognise that intervention is not always the most appropriate response.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section id="meaning" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">02 / UNDERSTANDING THE CONCEPT</p>
            <h2>What is conservation?</h2>

            <p>
              Conservation is the practice of safeguarding cultural
              heritage so that it can be understood, appreciated and
              accessed by present and future generations.
            </p>

            <p>
              It involves understanding the physical nature of
              objects and sites, their historical and cultural
              significance, the factors that affect their condition,
              and the choices available for their long-term care.
            </p>

            <p>
              Conservation is therefore not simply the repair of
              damaged objects. It includes preventive measures,
              scientific examination, documentation, research,
              treatment and the responsible management of heritage.
            </p>

            <div class="reference-note">
              <p class="note-label">PROFESSIONAL DEFINITION</p>
              <p>
                In its 2008 terminology resolution, ICOM-CC defines
                conservation as all measures and actions aimed at
                safeguarding tangible cultural heritage while
                ensuring its accessibility to present and future
                generations.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://www.icom-cc.org/en/terminology-for-conservation"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Read the ICOM-CC terminology resolution ↗
              </a>
            </div>

            <h3>Why does conservation matter?</h3>

            <p>
              Cultural heritage carries material evidence of human
              creativity, knowledge, belief, technology and social
              experience. Its significance may be historical,
              artistic, scientific, spiritual, social or cultural.
            </p>

            <p>
              When heritage is damaged, altered or lost, we may also
              lose information and connections that cannot easily
              be recovered. Conservation seeks to reduce that loss
              while respecting the significance and physical
              characteristics of the heritage concerned.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="approaches" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">03 / MAIN APPROACHES</p>
            <h2>Three approaches to conservation.</h2>

            <p>
              ICOM-CC identifies three broad categories within
              conservation. They are distinguished by the purpose
              of the measures and actions being undertaken.
            </p>

            <div class="approach">
              <span class="approach-number">01</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Preventive conservation</h3>
                <p>
                  Measures taken to avoid or minimise future
                  deterioration or loss. These actions generally
                  address the environment and circumstances
                  surrounding an object or collection rather
                  than directly changing the object.
                </p>
                <p class="example">
                  Examples: appropriate storage, handling,
                  environmental management, pest management,
                  security and emergency planning.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="approach">
              <span class="approach-number">02</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Remedial conservation</h3>
                <p>
                  Direct actions undertaken to arrest active
                  deterioration or strengthen an object when
                  its condition is sufficiently fragile or
                  deteriorating rapidly.
                </p>
                <p class="example">
                  Examples: stabilising corroded metal,
                  consolidating a fragile painted surface
                  or treating active deterioration in an object.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="approach">
              <span class="approach-number">03</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Restoration</h3>
                <p>
                  Direct actions on a stable object intended
                  to facilitate its appreciation, understanding
                  or use when its significance or function has
                  been affected by past alteration or deterioration.
                </p>
                <p class="example">
                  Examples: retouching losses in a painting
                  or reassembling a broken sculpture.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              These categories are not always mutually exclusive.
              A conservation project may involve more than one
              approach, depending on the condition, significance
              and needs of the object.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="scope" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">04 / SCOPE AND PRACTICE</p>
            <h2>The scope of conservation.</h2>

            <p>
              Conservation extends across a wide range of materials,
              objects, collections and heritage environments.
              Different materials behave differently, and their
              preservation requires an understanding of their
              composition, structure, history and condition.
            </p>

            <div class="scope-list">
              <div>
                <h3>Organic materials</h3>
                <p>
                  Paper, textiles, wood, leather, photographs
                  and other materials derived from living organisms.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3>Inorganic materials</h3>
                <p>
                  Stone, metals, ceramics, glass and other
                  mineral-based materials.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3>Composite objects</h3>
                <p>
                  Objects made from combinations of materials
                  that may respond differently to environmental
                  conditions and treatment.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3>Built and archaeological heritage</h3>
                <p>
                  Historic structures, monuments, archaeological
                  sites, architectural surfaces and cultural landscapes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p>
              The methods used in conservation must respond to
              the specific material and context. A treatment
              appropriate for one material may be unsuitable
              for another.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="decision-making" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">05 / PROFESSIONAL THINKING</p>
            <h2>Making conservation decisions.</h2>

            <p>
              Conservation decisions are rarely based on material
              condition alone. They require an understanding of
              what is significant about an object, what threatens
              it, what resources are available and what consequences
              a proposed action may have.
            </p>

            <p>
              Before deciding on a treatment, a conservator may
              need to consider:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>The historical, artistic, scientific and cultural significance of the object.</li>
              <li>Its material composition, structure and condition.</li>
              <li>The causes and extent of deterioration.</li>
              <li>Its current and intended use.</li>
              <li>The possible benefits and risks of intervention.</li>
              <li>Available expertise, facilities and resources.</li>
              <li>Future care, monitoring and maintenance.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="reference-note">
              <p class="note-label">A PRINCIPLE TO REMEMBER</p>
              <p>
                The decision to intervene should be supported
                by examination, investigation and a clear
                understanding of the expected outcome.
                Doing more does not necessarily mean conserving
                better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3>Conservation is collaborative.</h3>

            <p>
              Conservation often involves collaboration between
              conservators, curators, scientists, archaeologists,
              art historians, architects, collection managers,
              communities and other specialists.
            </p>

            <p>
              Different forms of expertise contribute to a fuller
              understanding of heritage and help ensure that
              decisions are informed by more than one perspective.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="reflection" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">06 / THINK AND REFLECT</p>
            <h2>Consider the situation.</h2>

            <div class="reflection">
              <p class="scenario-label">A CONSERVATION SCENARIO</p>
              <h3>
                A historic object shows signs of deterioration
                in a museum collection.
              </h3>
              <p>
                The museum is considering sending it for immediate
                treatment. However, the cause of deterioration
                has not yet been established.
              </p>
              <p>
                What should happen before a treatment decision
                is made?
              </p>
            </div>

            <h3>Questions for reflection</h3>
            <ol>
              <li>What information would you need before proposing a treatment?</li>
              <li>How would you investigate the cause of deterioration?</li>
              <li>Could preventive measures address the problem?</li>
              <li>Who should be involved in the decision-making process?</li>
              <li>What might be the consequences of intervening without sufficient investigation?</li>
            </ol>

            <p>
              There is no single treatment answer to this scenario.
              The purpose is to develop a habit of investigation
              and reasoned decision-making before action.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="references" class="lesson-section">
            <p class="section-label">07 / REFERENCES AND FURTHER READING</p>
            <h2>Continue exploring.</h2>

            <div class="reference-item">
              <h3>ICOM-CC (2008)</h3>
              <p>
                Terminology to Characterize the Conservation
                of Tangible Cultural Heritage.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://www.icom-cc.org/en/terminology-for-conservation"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Access the terminology ↗
              </a>
            </div>

            <div class="reference-item">
              <h3>American Institute for Conservation</h3>
              <p>
                Code of Ethics and Guidelines for Practice.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://www.culturalheritage.org/conservation-at-work/uphold-professional-standards/code"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Read the professional code ↗
              </a>
            </div>

            <div class="reference-item">
              <h3>Canadian Conservation Institute</h3>
              <p>
                Agents of Deterioration: an introduction to
                the principal threats to heritage collections.
              </p>
              <a
                href="https://www.canada.ca/en/conservation-institute/services/agents-deterioration.html"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                Explore the agents of deterioration ↗
              </a>
            </div>
          </section>

          <ModuleQuiz />

          <nav class="lesson-navigation">
            <a href="/conservation/">← Back to Conservation</a>
            <span>MODULE 01 / END</span>
          </nav>

        </div>
      </section>

    </article>
  </Container>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lesson-page {
    padding-top: 0.5rem;
